From: Eduardo Garcia-Madico Portabella <woodyst@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 16 Aug 2026 12:10:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] pinctrl: qcom: clear a stale parent irq when muxing a pin back
 to GPIO

msm_pinmux_set_mux() already knows that muxing a pin away from GPIO and
back can leave a bogus edge latched: the detection logic keeps watching
the pad while the pin is doing something else. It masks the interrupt on
the way out and, on the way back in, clears whatever got latched and
unmasks again.

The clearing is gated on having done the masking:

	if (d && i == gpio_func &&
	    test_and_clear_bit(d->hwirq, pctrl->disabled_for_mux)) {

and that gate never opens for a pin that was muxed away *before* its
interrupt existed. pinctrl_bind_pins() runs from really_probe() before
drv->probe(), so a driver that maps its wakeup interrupt inside probe()
- qcom_geni_serial does exactly this, via platform_get_irq_optional()
and dev_pm_set_dedicated_wake_irq() - gets its default pinctrl state
applied while irq_find_mapping() still returns 0. d is NULL, nothing is
masked, disabled_for_mux stays clear, and from then on every mux back to
GPIO skips the clear.

On SoCs where the pin is a PDC wakeup GPIO that is not a cosmetic
problem, because the latch lives in the PDC and survives everything the
TLMM does. Measured on an SM7150 phone (Xiaomi POCO X3 NFC), where GPIO
41 is the Bluetooth UART RX line, is listed in the SoC PDC map as
{41, 101}, and carries the UART's dedicated wake interrupt:

    interrupts-extended = <&intc GIC_SPI 604 IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_HIGH>,
                          <&tlmm 41 IRQ_TYPE_EDGE_FALLING>;

    qup-uart3-default-state: rx-pins  function = "qup03"  bias-pull-up
    qup-uart3-sleep-state:   rx-pins  function = "gpio"   bias-pull-up

Every system suspend muxes that pin to GPIO and arms the wake interrupt
at the same time, the stale PDC latch fires immediately, and the machine
never sleeps:

    pmirq: (pm_system_irq_wakeup+0x0/0xbc) irq=158
    irq 158 -> msmgpio 41 Edge  88c000.serial:wakeup

    1604.990895  pm_system_irq_wakeup(158)     <- 151 us before
    1604.991046  dpm_suspend_noirq begin
    1604.996430  dpm_suspend_noirq end
    1604.996435  dpm_resume_noirq begin

There is no machine_suspend phase at all: suspend_enter() finds
pm_wakeup_pending() already true after dpm_suspend_noirq() and unwinds
with -EBUSY. The device never sleeps for a microsecond. It is not a race
with real UART traffic either - the port sees 0.4 interrupts/s and the
controller sends one HCI event per ten seconds, yet sixteen consecutive
suspend attempts failed the same way.

Clearing the latch does not depend on having masked the interrupt, so
stop gating it. Any edge latched while the pin was not a GPIO is bogus
by construction, which is what the existing comment already says. Only
the unmask needs to stay paired with the mask.

With this, the same phone suspends for the full requested interval, and
re-enabling the wake path puts it back to one second - so the mechanism
is confirmed from both sides.

Signed-off-by: Eduardo Garcia-Madico Portabella <woodyst@gmail.com>
---
 drivers/pinctrl/qcom/pinctrl-msm.c | 18 +++++++++++++-----
 1 file changed, 15 insertions(+), 4 deletions(-)

--- a/drivers/pinctrl/qcom/pinctrl-msm.c
+++ b/drivers/pinctrl/qcom/pinctrl-msm.c
@@ -244,18 +244,30 @@
 
 	raw_spin_unlock_irqrestore(&pctrl->lock, flags);
 
-	if (d && i == gpio_func &&
-	    test_and_clear_bit(d->hwirq, pctrl->disabled_for_mux)) {
+	if (d && i == gpio_func) {
+		bool was_masked = test_and_clear_bit(d->hwirq,
+						     pctrl->disabled_for_mux);
+
 		/*
 		 * Clear interrupts detected while not GPIO since we only
 		 * masked things.
+		 *
+		 * This has to happen even when we never masked anything
+		 * ourselves. A pin can be muxed away before its interrupt is
+		 * mapped at all -- pinctrl_bind_pins() runs before probe(), so
+		 * a driver that maps a wakeup interrupt in probe() misses the
+		 * mask above and disabled_for_mux never gets set. Where the pin
+		 * is a PDC wakeup GPIO the bogus edge is latched in the PDC and
+		 * nothing else will ever clear it.
 		 */
 		if (d->parent_data && test_bit(d->hwirq, pctrl->skip_wake_irqs))
 			irq_chip_set_parent_state(d, IRQCHIP_STATE_PENDING, false);
 		else
 			msm_ack_intr_status(pctrl, g);
 
-		enable_irq(irq);
+		/* only the unmask has to stay paired with the mask */
+		if (was_masked)
+			enable_irq(irq);
 	}
 
 	return 0;
